D1 and D3 admit the SAME chains once zero operands are excluded. checked:
  cells checked: 42.  cells where they differ: 0
  and it is a theorem, not a coincidence. write T_i for the total 2-adic valuation of
  the start and the first i multipliers. D1 admits iff T_i >= i*F for every i; D3 admits
  iff T_k >= k*F. a nonzero raw below 2^F has valuation at most F-1, so if T_i <= i*F - 1
  then T_k <= i*F - 1 + (k-i)(F-1) = k*F - 1 - (k-i) < k*F. contrapositive: T_k >= k*F
  forces every T_i >= i*F. the hypothesis is exactly W == F, which is this file's domain.

what the numbers say, stated as what they are and no further:
  * refusal on inexact is not a viable design for chains, and the number is not close.
    at F=8 with nonzero operands it admits 1.18% of single multiplies, 0.0062% of pairs,
    and 0.000018% of triples, which is eighteen chains in a hundred million. a strategy
    whose intent is to be accurate WITHIN CHAINS cannot be one that refuses all of them.
  * where the two refusal designs were presented as a choice, they are the same policy.
    the 1.5x to 4.5x gap in the with-zero columns is entirely chains containing a zero
    operand, which are exact by accident.
  * so the fork 45 names is not two live designs. one arm is measurable and is not a
    design anyone ships. what survives as a real question is narrower and is about the
    container derivation directly: whether the wide value a multiply forms internally is
    CARRIED between operations, or consumed inside one and discarded. every fixed-point
    multiply forms a 2W-bit product whatever the strategy, so forming it is not what
    distinguishes Precise; carrying it is.

what this file does not establish:
  * the W > F case. the equivalence theorem above needs every operand's valuation to be
    at most F-1, which holds exactly when W == F. with integer bits present a raw can
    carry more trailing zeros than F, the implication breaks, and D1 and D3 may separate.
    a design keyed on total-and-fraction width would want that measured; I did not.
  * 18 section 3.4's 4.60% to 55.56% is a different domain (in-range multiplications and
    divisions over mixed integer and fraction widths). these numbers do not replace it
    and are not comparable to it row for row.
  * what any of the three designs costs in cycles or code size. no bench in this panel
    has priced it, so it is unpriced.
